**[P1, not introduced by this MR] A sheath holding a weapon in a house crashes the server at the next house save**

**Location:** [`objsave_save_obj_record_db_sheath()`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/obj/objsave.c#L4692-4705): `GET_NAME(ch)` at 4699, and again in the log line this MR rewrote (4703-4705). It is reached with `ch == NULL` from `House_save()` ([`house.c:131`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/obj/house.c#L131)) through [`objsave.c:606-616`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/obj/objsave.c#L606-616).

**Defect.** House objects are written with `ch == NULL`. The record writer branches on that for its own `INSERT`, but hands the NULL to the sheath writer, which dereferences it. The crash is at base `ec8f55b26` too (line 4691 there). It is reported here because the MR edits both functions, changes the sheath writer's result handling, and adds house-save tests, none of which has a sheath.

**Repro in play** (traced in the code, not played). Any player who can stand in a house room:

1. Wear a sheath, wield a weapon, `sheath`.
2. `remove` the sheath. Nothing empties it or refuses: no check exists in `act.item.c`.
3. Enter a house room and `drop` the sheath. `obj_to_room()` sets `ROOM_HOUSE_CRASH`.
4. `save`. `perform_save()` calls `House_crashsave()` for a flagged house room ([`act.other.c:5744-5745`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/act/act.other.c#L5744-5745)). Without the `save`, the next house-save pass does it.

**Repro in a test** (run on a25b937e9, MariaDB 10.11; a sheath with a weapon in it on a house floor, then `House_crashsave()`):

```
Program received signal SIGSEGV, Segmentation fault.
#0  objsave_save_obj_record_db_sheath (obj=..., ch=0x0, sheath_idnum=2, sheath_slot=1) at src/obj/objsave.c:4699
#1  objsave_save_obj_record_internal (obj=..., ch=0x0, house_vnum=91399, ...)
#2  objsave_save_obj_record_db (...) at src/obj/objsave.c:152
#3  House_save (...) at src/obj/house.c:131
#5  House_crashsave (vnum=91399) at src/obj/house.c:200
```

<details><summary>Probe</summary>

```c
void Test_mr20_probe_house_sheath(CuTest *tc)
{
  struct transactions_world w;
  struct obj_data *sheath;

  if (!transactions_world_begin(tc, &w))
    return;

  sheath = saves_furnish(&w, "a sheath");
  SET_BIT_AR(GET_OBJ_WEAR(sheath), ITEM_WEAR_SHEATH);
  sheath->sheath_primary = read_object(SAVES_OBJECT_VNUM, VIRTUAL);
  House_crashsave(SAVES_HOUSE_VNUM); /* SIGSEGV */
  extract_obj(sheath->sheath_primary);
  sheath->sheath_primary = NULL;
  transactions_world_end(tc, &w);
}
```

</details>

**Fix.** Two parts, since a guard alone would lose the weapon: `player_save_objs_sheathed` rows are loaded by owner name, and a house has none.

- Do not let a loaded sheath leave its wearer: on `remove`, return the sheathed weapons to the inventory (or refuse while it is loaded).
- Guard the writer anyway: with `ch == NULL`, skip the sheath rows and log it, instead of dereferencing.

**[P2] Quit, idle and cryo saves: a lost transaction or an unanswered COMMIT leaves the inventory in memory, and it is dropped in the room beside a save that still holds it**

**Location:** [`objsave_commit_player_save()`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/obj/objsave.c#L1344-1359), used by `Crash_idlesave()` (1525), `Crash_rentsave()` (1597) and `Crash_cryosave()` (1697).

**Defect.** This new helper takes every failed `COMMIT` for a rollback, which is the case the description says is gone. Only `Crash_crashsave()` has an answer for it (the flag stays, the next pass rewrites). The other three return before `Crash_extract_objs(ch->carrying)`. Their callers go straight on to `extract_char()` ([`do_quit`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/act/act.other.c#L5690-5703), [idle-out](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/core/limits.c#L1427-1432)), and `extract_char()` puts everything the character still carries on the floor ([`handler.c:3303-3311`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/core/handler.c#L3303-3311)).

Before this MR a drop inside a quit save usually ended in a "successful" `COMMIT` of a partial save. Now every drop inside one takes this branch, so the floor drop is the normal outcome.

**Repro** (a25b937e9, MariaDB 10.11, probe appended to `test_database_transactions.c`):

```
PROBE rent, lost at a row:      rows in database=1, objects still carried=2
PROBE rent, COMMIT unanswered:  rows in database=2, objects still carried=2
```

- Lost at a row: the database keeps the previous save (one gem), both gems go to the floor. The first gem now exists twice.
- `COMMIT` run, reply lost: the database holds both gems and both go to the floor. The whole inventory exists twice.

<details><summary>Probe</summary>

```c
void Test_mr20_probe_rent(CuTest *tc)
{
  struct transactions_world w;
  struct obj_data *obj;
  int rows, carried;

  if (!transactions_world_begin(tc, &w))
    return;

  saves_carry(&w, "a first gem");
  CuAssertTrue(tc, Crash_crashsave(&w.owner));
  saves_carry(&w, "a second gem");
  mysql_test_drop_connection_at("INSERT INTO player_save_objs", 1, FALSE);
  Crash_rentsave(&w.owner, 0);
  rows = saves_player_rows(&w);
  for (carried = 0, obj = w.owner.carrying; obj != NULL; obj = obj->next_content)
    carried++;
  printf("\nPROBE rent, lost at a row: rows in database=%d, objects still carried=%d\n", rows,
         carried);

  mysql_test_drop_connection_at("commit", 1, TRUE);
  Crash_rentsave(&w.owner, 0);
  rows = saves_player_rows(&w);
  for (carried = 0, obj = w.owner.carrying; obj != NULL; obj = obj->next_content)
    carried++;
  printf("PROBE rent, COMMIT unanswered: rows in database=%d, objects still carried=%d\n", rows,
         carried);

  transactions_world_end(tc, &w);
}
```

</details>

**Fix.**

1. Commit with `mysql_commit_transaction()`. When it is not `MYSQL_COMMIT_DONE`, run the whole save once more on the reconnected session. The save is absolute (`DELETE` then `INSERT`), so writing again is safe, as `trade_record()` already does.
2. A second attempt needs the worn gear still in memory: move the per-slot `Crash_extract_objs(GET_EQ(ch, j))` (1513, 1583, 1687) to after the confirmed `COMMIT`.
3. If the second attempt fails too, still extract what the character holds and tell the staff. The player then keeps the last save instead of a duplicate. The early returns after a failed `objsave_begin_player_save()` or `objsave_write_rentcode()` need the same treatment.

**[P2] Cryo and idle saves now delete the bag rows and never write them back (`free_rent = NO`)**

**Location:** [`Crash_cryosave()`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/obj/objsave.c#L1655-1697) (the new `objsave_begin_player_save()` at 1657) and the empty path of [`Crash_idlesave()`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/obj/objsave.c#L1479-1491) (the `rollback` that became a commit at 1488).

**Defect.** Both changes are from this MR.

- `Crash_cryosave()` now deletes every `player_save_objs` row of the player, then writes worn gear and `ch->carrying` and commits. It never calls `Crash_save_bags()`, unlike the crash, idle and rent saves (1407, 1517, 1587). Before, nothing was deleted, so the bag rows of the last crash save survived.
- `Crash_idlesave()` tests only `ch->carrying` and the worn slots for "nothing to store", then commits the delete. Before, this path rolled back, so the rows stayed.

Bag items live only in `ch->bags->bagN`. `extract_char()` then unlinks them (`empty_bags_to_inventory()`), so they are gone from memory and from the database.

**Repro** (a25b937e9, MariaDB 10.11; one gem carried, one gem in bag 1):

```
PROBE bags cryo: rows before=2 after=1, bag1 still holds a bagged gem
PROBE bags idle: rows before=1 after=0, bag1 still holds a bagged gem
```

In play, with `free_rent = NO`: `sortto` an item into a bag and use the cryogenicist, or idle out wearing and carrying nothing with items sorted into bags. The bagged items are missing at the next login. Unreachable while rent is free, which is the default.

<details><summary>Probe</summary>

```c
void Test_mr20_probe_bags(CuTest *tc)
{
  struct transactions_world w;
  struct obj_data *bagged;
  int before, after_cryo, before_idle, after_idle;

  if (!transactions_world_begin(tc, &w))
    return;

  saves_carry(&w, "a carried gem");
  bagged = read_object(SAVES_OBJECT_VNUM, VIRTUAL);
  bagged->short_description = strdup("a bagged gem");
  GET_OBJ_SORT(bagged) = 1;
  w.bags.bag1 = bagged;

  CuAssertTrue(tc, Crash_crashsave(&w.owner));
  before = saves_player_rows(&w);
  Crash_cryosave(&w.owner, 0);
  after_cryo = saves_player_rows(&w);
  REMOVE_BIT_AR(PLR_FLAGS(&w.owner), PLR_CRYO);
  printf("\nPROBE bags cryo: rows before=%d after=%d, bag1 still holds %s\n", before, after_cryo,
         w.bags.bag1 != NULL ? w.bags.bag1->short_description : "(nothing)");

  /* Nothing carried or worn, one gem in a bag. */
  CuAssertPtrEquals(tc, NULL, w.owner.carrying);
  CuAssertTrue(tc, Crash_crashsave(&w.owner));
  before_idle = saves_player_rows(&w);
  Crash_idlesave(&w.owner);
  after_idle = saves_player_rows(&w);
  printf("PROBE bags idle: rows before=%d after=%d, bag1 still holds %s\n", before_idle,
         after_idle, w.bags.bag1 != NULL ? w.bags.bag1->short_description : "(nothing)");

  w.bags.bag1 = NULL;
  extract_obj(bagged);
  transactions_world_end(tc, &w);
}
```

</details>

**Fix.**

- `Crash_cryosave()`: add `unsaved += Crash_save_bags(ch, fp);` before the inventory save. Checked: with that line the cryo probe reads `rows before=2 after=2`.
- `Crash_idlesave()`: count the ten bag lists in the emptiness test, or drop the early return so line 1517 runs.

**[P2] A deadlock victim loses its transaction without the mark: the statements after it commit on their own and `COMMIT` reports success**

**Location:** [`transaction_watch_end()`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/database/mysql.c#L184-196).

**Defect.** On error 1213 InnoDB rolls back the whole transaction, not the statement, and the connection stays up. An error packet carries no status flags, so `SERVER_STATUS_IN_TRANS` stays set on the client, `still_open` is true, and the connection is not marked. From there it is the failure this MR removes for a lost connection: every later statement runs in autocommit, and `COMMIT` returns `MYSQL_COMMIT_DONE` over nothing.

It matters more after this MR than before, because the writers now go on after a failure by design: `vessel_db_save_cargo()` lost its in-transaction stop, and the object and house writers count the failed row and continue.

**Repro** (a25b937e9, MariaDB 10.11). The game's transaction writes row 1, is chosen as the victim on its second write, writes row 4 and commits:

```
PROBE deadlock: failed_rc=1 errno=1213 later_rc=0 commit=0 (0=DONE) row1=0 row4=7
```

Row 1 was rolled back, row 4 was committed alone, and the helper reported the transaction committed.

At the sites: an object or house save keeps the old rows (the `DELETE` is rolled back) beside the rows written after the victim, so a rent save loads duplicates at the next login. A cargo save leaves duplicate lots, since `ship_cargo_manifest` has no unique key.

**Trigger.** It needs a second session that locks the same rows. The game's single thread cannot deadlock with itself, so this takes an outside writer: administrative SQL, tooling, or a second game on the same database. That is uncommon, but the damage is the same as the lost connection's.

<details><summary>Probe</summary>

```c
void Test_mr20_probe_deadlock_victim(CuTest *tc)
{
  struct transactions_database database;
  MYSQL *other;
  int failed_rc, later_rc;
  unsigned int failed_errno;
  enum mysql_commit_result commit;
  const char *blocked = "UPDATE " TRANSACTIONS_TABLE " SET v = 9 WHERE id = 1";

  if (!transactions_begin(tc, &database))
    return;
  transactions_table(tc, conn);
  CuAssertIntEquals(tc, 0, mysql_query(conn, "INSERT INTO " TRANSACTIONS_TABLE
                                             " VALUES (2, 0), (3, 0), (4, 0)"));
  other = transactions_connect();
  CuAssertPtrNotNull(tc, other);

  /* The other session is the heavier transaction: it holds rows 2 and 3. */
  CuAssertIntEquals(tc, 0, (mysql_query)(other, "START TRANSACTION"));
  CuAssertIntEquals(tc, 0, (mysql_query)(other, "UPDATE " TRANSACTIONS_TABLE
                                                " SET v = 9 WHERE id IN (2, 3)"));

  CuAssertIntEquals(tc, 0, mysql_query(conn, "START TRANSACTION"));
  CuAssertIntEquals(tc, 0, mysql_query(conn, "UPDATE " TRANSACTIONS_TABLE " SET v = 1 WHERE id = 1"));

  /* The other session now waits for row 1. */
  CuAssertIntEquals(tc, 0, mysql_send_query(other, blocked, (unsigned long)strlen(blocked)));
  usleep(500000);

  /* The game's second write closes the cycle: it is the victim. */
  failed_rc = mysql_query(conn, "UPDATE " TRANSACTIONS_TABLE " SET v = 1 WHERE id = 2");
  failed_errno = mysql_errno(conn);
  later_rc = mysql_query(conn, "UPDATE " TRANSACTIONS_TABLE " SET v = 7 WHERE id = 4");
  commit = mysql_commit_transaction(conn);

  (void)mysql_read_query_result(other);
  (mysql_query)(other, "ROLLBACK");
  mysql_close(other);

  printf("\nPROBE deadlock: failed_rc=%d errno=%u later_rc=%d commit=%d (0=DONE) row1=%d row4=%d\n",
         failed_rc, failed_errno, later_rc, (int)commit,
         transactions_value(conn, "SELECT v FROM " TRANSACTIONS_TABLE " WHERE id = 1"),
         transactions_value(conn, "SELECT v FROM " TRANSACTIONS_TABLE " WHERE id = 4"));
  mysql_query(conn, "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS " TRANSACTIONS_TABLE);
  transactions_end(&database);
}
```

</details>

**Fix.** Treat `ER_LOCK_DEADLOCK` as a lost transaction in `transaction_watch_end()`:

```c
  if (!watch.open || (error != CR_SERVER_GONE_ERROR && error != CR_SERVER_LOST &&
                      error != ER_LOCK_DEADLOCK && (error == 0 || still_open) &&
                      mysql_thread_id(mysql_conn) == watch.session))
    return;
```

Checked: with that change the probe reads `later_rc=1 commit=1 row4=0` (the later write is refused, the commit is `MYSQL_COMMIT_REFUSED`), and the ten `Test_database_*` cases still pass. The log line's wording ("lost with its connection") would need the deadlock case added.

**[P2] An unanswered `COMMIT` while the database is away: the event is scored twice on the retry the staff are told to make**

**Location:** [`vessel_event_finish()`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/vessels/vessels_events.c#L748-773). The same shape is in `pet_store_pet()` ([`players.c:7917-7927`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/player/players.c#L7917-7927)) and `pet_retrieve_stored()` ([`players.c:8144-8151`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/player/players.c#L8144-8151)).

**Defect.** The description lists a failed read-back as out of scope. It is the usual case, though: a `COMMIT` reply is mostly lost to a server restart or crash, and then the single immediate read-back cannot connect either. `vessel_event_database_status_is()` returns FALSE on any failure, the code takes that for a rollback, and:

- sets `recovery_required` and tells the staff to retry `VEVENT END`. The retry runs the leaderboard increments again (`entries=entries+1, wins=wins+VALUES(wins), points=points+VALUES(points)`, line 591) with no check of the row;
- writes `recovery_failed` over the event row with no predicate (line 768). If the database is back by then (the read-back failed on a lock wait, say), this erases the `completed` marker itself.

**Repro** (a25b937e9, MariaDB 10.11). The `COMMIT` is run and its reply lost, reconnects are refused during `VEVENT END` (the handle's port is pointed away, then put back), and the staff member retries:

```
PROBE event: after END: staff heard [The event remains open ... retry] leaderboard entries=1 status=completed
PROBE event: after the retry: leaderboard entries=2 (one event was played)
```

The pet sites guess the same way. A store whose `COMMIT` landed leaves a STORED row beside the live pet, so the pet and its gear exist twice (or clash on a later save). A retrieve whose `COMMIT` landed leaves an ACTIVE row with no live pet, which the next pet save deletes with its gear.

<details><summary>Probe</summary>

```c
void Test_mr20_probe_event_double_score(CuTest *tc)
{
  struct transactions_world w;
  struct greyhawk_ship_data *ship;
  unsigned int port;
  char heard[512];
  int entries_after_end, entries_after_retry;
  char status_after_end[64];

  if (!transactions_world_begin(tc, &w))
    return;
  vessel_event_ensure_schema();
  mysql_query(conn, "DELETE FROM vessel_event_leaderboards WHERE player_idnum = 4314");
  mysql_query(conn, "DELETE FROM vessel_showcase_events WHERE staff_idnum = 4314");
  ship = transactions_hull_begin(tc);
  strlcpy(ship->owner, GET_NAME(&w.staff), sizeof(ship->owner));
  ship->bridge_room = SAVES_HOUSE_VNUM;
  w.room.ship = ship;
  IN_ROOM(&w.staff) = 0;
  GET_IDNUM(&w.staff) = 4314;

  do_vevent(&w.staff, "start skirmish", 0, 0);
  do_vevent(&w.staff, "join red", 0, 0);
  transactions_staff_heard(&w);
  mysql_test_drop_connection_at("COMMIT", 1, TRUE);
  port = conn->port;
  conn->port = 1; /* no reconnect succeeds: the database is away */
  do_vevent(&w.staff, "end", 0, 0);
  conn->port = port; /* and it is back */
  strlcpy(heard, transactions_staff_heard(&w), sizeof(heard));
  entries_after_end = transactions_event_entries();
  strlcpy(status_after_end,
          transactions_text(conn, "SELECT status FROM vessel_showcase_events WHERE "
                                  "staff_idnum = 4314 ORDER BY event_id DESC LIMIT 1"),
          sizeof(status_after_end));
  printf("\nPROBE event: after END: staff heard [%.120s] leaderboard entries=%d status=%s\n",
         strstr(heard, "The event remains open") != NULL ? "The event remains open ... retry"
                                                         : heard,
         entries_after_end, status_after_end);

  do_vevent(&w.staff, "end", 0, 0);
  entries_after_retry = transactions_event_entries();
  printf("PROBE event: after the retry: leaderboard entries=%d (one event was played)\n",
         entries_after_retry);

  mysql_query(conn, "DELETE FROM vessel_event_leaderboards WHERE player_idnum = 4314");
  mysql_query(conn, "DELETE FROM vessel_showcase_events WHERE staff_idnum = 4314");
  w.room.ship = NULL;
  transactions_hull_end();
  transactions_world_end(tc, &w);
}
```

</details>

**Fix.**

- Event: make the finish idempotent on its own row. Inside the transaction, run the status `UPDATE` first with `AND status NOT IN ('completed','cancelled')`, and write the leaderboard rows only when it changed a row. With no row changed, commit and clear the event in memory. Give the `recovery_failed` write at 768 the same predicate.
- Pets: do not choose a state when the read-back could not run. Keep the outcome pending and read the row again before that owner's next pet save or keeper visit. If a guess must stay, "stored" is the safe one for `pet_store_pet()`: the pet leaves play and no second copy exists.

**[P3] An incomplete save keeps the crash flag but never sets it, so a save made while the flag was clear is not retried**

**Location:** [`Crash_crashsave()`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/obj/objsave.c#L1418-1423) and [`House_crashsave()`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/obj/house.c#L212-218).

**Defect.** The description says an incomplete save "keeps `PLR_CRASH` or `ROOM_HOUSE_CRASH` so the next pass saves again". The flag is only left alone. Several callers save with it clear: `perform_save()` (the `save` command, [`act.other.c:5743`](https://gitlab.com/max757/Luminari-Source/-/blob/a25b937e9f9be67473dc3312a3d582e196926d05/src/act/act.other.c#L5743)), the crafting saves, and the direct `House_crashsave()` calls in `house.c`. There the delete and the other rows are committed, the refused object's old row is gone, and `Crash_save_incremental()`, `Crash_save_all()` and the house pass skip the unflagged player or room. A crash or reboot before the next inventory change loses the object.

**Repro** (a25b937e9, MariaDB 10.11). Two gems saved, flag clear, then the database refuses one row and the player saves:

```
PROBE flag: save returned 0, rows=1, PLR_CRASH=0
```

<details><summary>Probe</summary>

```c
void Test_mr20_probe_flag(CuTest *tc)
{
  struct transactions_world w;
  struct obj_data *gem;
  bool saved;

  if (!transactions_world_begin(tc, &w))
    return;
  gem = saves_carry(&w, "a plain gem");
  saves_carry(&w, "a second gem");
  CuAssertTrue(tc, Crash_crashsave(&w.owner));
  CuAssertTrue(tc, !PLR_FLAGGED(&w.owner, PLR_CRASH));
  CuAssertIntEquals(tc, 2, saves_player_rows(&w));

  /* The database now refuses the first gem's row; the player types save. */
  CuAssertIntEquals(tc, 0,
                    mysql_query(conn, "ALTER TABLE player_save_objs ADD CONSTRAINT s13_refused "
                                      "CHECK (serialized_obj NOT LIKE '%refused%')"));
  free(gem->short_description);
  gem->short_description = strdup("a refused gem");
  saved = Crash_crashsave(&w.owner);
  printf("\nPROBE flag: save returned %d, rows=%d, PLR_CRASH=%d\n", (int)saved,
         saves_player_rows(&w), PLR_FLAGGED(&w.owner, PLR_CRASH) ? 1 : 0);
  transactions_world_end(tc, &w);
}
```

</details>

**Fix.** Set the flag on every failed or incomplete return that comes after the delete: `SET_BIT_AR(PLR_FLAGS(ch), PLR_CRASH)` in `Crash_crashsave()`, `SET_BIT_AR(ROOM_FLAGS(rnum), ROOM_HOUSE_CRASH)` in `House_crashsave()`. The MR's own test sets `PLR_CRASH` by hand before the refused save, which is why it passes.
